\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Background}
A triple $(a,b,c)$ of positive integers with $a^2+b^2=c^2$ and $\gcd(a,b)=1$ is a \emph{primitive Pythagorean triple}. In such a triple exactly one leg is odd and the hypotenuse is odd; we adopt throughout the normalisation that the odd leg comes first.

Berggren observed in 1934 that the three integer matrices
\[
\mathbf{A}=\begin{pmatrix} 1 & -2 & 2\\ 2 & -1 & 2\\ 2 & -2 & 3\end{pmatrix},\quad
\mathbf{B}=\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2 & 2\\ 2 & 1 & 2\\ 2 & 2 & 3\end{pmatrix},\quad
\mathbf{C}=\begin{pmatrix} -1 & 2 & 2\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\ -2 & 2 & 3\end{pmatrix}
\]
map primitive triples to primitive triples, and that iterating them from the seed $(3,4,5)$ produces every primitive triple exactly once. The resulting structure is an infinite ternary tree.

The \emph{forward} half of this statement (the moves preserve the class) is a routine algebraic verification. The \emph{descent} half --- every primitive triple has a unique parent in the tree --- is the substantive content, and it is usually proved by a three-way case analysis on inequalities. Our first contribution is a case-free treatment.

\subsection{The logical questions}
A structure with canonical well-founded descent is the natural substrate for two phenomena from mathematical logic.

\textbf{(Q1) Undecidability.} Matiyasevich's theorem provides undecidable Diophantine relations. Since the address relation of the Berggren tree, $\addr(w)=(a,b,c)$, is a relation between words and integer triples defined by iterated integer-linear maps, one might hope to encode a universal Diophantine machine in it, giving an undecidable first-order theory attached to the tree.

\textbf{(Q2) Independence.} Goodstein's theorem, the Kirby--Paris hydra and the Paris--Harrington principle are true statements about finite combinatorial objects that are unprovable in Peano Arithmetic ($\mathrm{PA}$). Each is calibrated by an ordinal: they exhaust $\varepsilon_0=\sup\{\omega,\omega^\omega,\omega^{\omega^\omega},\dots\}$, the proof-theoretic ordinal of $\mathrm{PA}$. Does the Berggren descent support a hydra game whose termination is unprovable in $\mathrm{PA}$?

We answer both questions negatively, and in each case the negative answer is a precise theorem: the tree is \emph{free} with a computable address bijection (\S4), and the associated hydra game has an \emph{exactly computed elementary length function} placing it at $\omega^\omega$ (\S5--\S6).

\subsection{Summary of results}
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Uniform descent} (Theorems~\ref{thm:parenteqinv}--\ref{thm:childrec}): one formula $P$ realises all three inverse Berggren moves; it preserves primitivity and strictly reduces the hypotenuse.
\item \textbf{Classification and decidability} (Theorem~\ref{thm:classification}, Corollary~\ref{cor:decid}).
\item \textbf{Freeness} (Theorem~\ref{thm:free}); answer to (Q1): no.
\item \textbf{Termination} of the Pythagorean Hydra (Theorem~\ref{thm:terminates}).
\item \textbf{Exact length} (Theorem~\ref{thm:length}); answer to (Q2): no, the game is at $\omega^\omega$.
\item \textbf{Sharpness of descent} (Theorems~\ref{thm:relaxed}, \ref{thm:children}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Definitions and normal form}

\begin{definition}
For $a,b,c\in\Z$ write $\Pyth(a,b,c)$ for $a^2+b^2=c^2$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
$(a,b,c)$ is a \emph{normalised primitive triple}, written $\PPT(a,b,c)$, if
\[ a>0,\quad b>0,\quad c>0,\quad a^2+b^2=c^2,\quad \gcd(a,b)=1,\quad a\text{ odd}. \]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Berggren moves]
\[
\begin{aligned}
A(a,b,c) &= (\,a-2b+2c,\ 2a-b+2c,\ 2a-2b+3c\,),\\
B(a,b,c) &= (\,a+2b+2c,\ 2a+b+2c,\ 2a+2b+3c\,),\\
C(a,b,c) &= (-a+2b+2c,\ -2a+b+2c,\ -2a+2b+3c\,),
\end{aligned}
\]
with formal inverses
\[
\begin{aligned}
A^{-1}(a,b,c) &= (\,a+2b-2c,\ -2a-b+2c,\ -2a-2b+3c\,),\\
B^{-1}(a,b,c) &= (\,a+2b-2c,\ \ 2a+b-2c,\ -2a-2b+3c\,),\\
C^{-1}(a,b,c) &= (-a-2b+2c,\ \ 2a+b-2c,\ -2a-2b+3c\,).
\end{aligned}
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
$\Reach$ is the least class of integer triples containing $(3,4,5)$ and closed under $A$, $B$, $C$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Parity]\label{lem:parity}
If $\PPT(a,b,c)$ then $b$ is even and $c$ is odd.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If both $a$ and $b$ were odd, then $c^2\equiv 2\pmod 4$, impossible since squares are $0$ or $1$ mod $4$; coprimality forbids both even, so $b$ is even. Then $c^2=a^2+b^2$ is odd, so $c$ is odd.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:legs}
If $\PPT(a,b,c)$ then $a<c$ and $b<c$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$c^2=a^2+b^2>a^2$ with all quantities positive.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Divisibility transfer]\label{lem:divtransfer}
If $\Pyth(a,b,c)$, $d\mid a$ and $d\mid b$, then $d\mid c$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write $a=dk_1$, $b=dk_2$; then $c^2=d^2(k_1^2+k_2^2)$, so $d^2\mid c^2$ and $d\mid c$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Coprimality transfer]\label{lem:coptransfer}
Let $\Pyth(a',b',c')$ and $\gcd(a,b)=1$, and suppose there are integers $p,q,r,s,t,w$ with
$a=pa'+qb'+rc'$ and $b=sa'+tb'+wc'$. Then $\gcd(a',b')=1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Let $g=\gcd(a',b')$. Then $g\mid c'$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:divtransfer}, so $g$ divides both displayed combinations, i.e.\ $g\mid\gcd(a,b)=1$.
\end{proof}

Lemma~\ref{lem:coptransfer} is the workhorse: every preservation-of-primitivity statement below is an instance of it, because each Berggren move and its inverse are integer-linear with integer-linear inverses.

\begin{proposition}[Forward moves preserve the class]\label{prop:forward}
If $\PPT(a,b,c)$ then $\PPT$ holds of $A(a,b,c)$, $B(a,b,c)$ and $C(a,b,c)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Positivity: for $A$, $a-2b+2c>a>0$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:legs}, and $2a-b+2c>0$ likewise; symmetric arguments handle $B$ and $C$. The Pythagorean identity is a polynomial identity, e.g.\
\[ (a-2b+2c)^2+(2a-b+2c)^2-(2a-2b+3c)^2 = a^2+b^2-c^2 = 0 . \]
Coprimality follows from Lemma~\ref{lem:coptransfer} with the inverse combinations read off from $A^{-1}$, $B^{-1}$, $C^{-1}$. Oddness of the first entry: each new first coordinate is $\pm a$ plus an even number.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Minimality of the root]\label{lem:root}
If $\PPT(a,b,c)$ and $c\le 5$ then $(a,b,c)=(3,4,5)$. Consequently $c\ge 5$ always.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Finite check over $c\le 5$, $a,b<c$, $a$ odd, $a^2+b^2=c^2$, $\gcd(a,b)=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Forward moves increase the hypotenuse]\label{lem:hypup}
If $\PPT(a,b,c)$ then the hypotenuse of each of $A(a,b,c)$, $B(a,b,c)$, $C(a,b,c)$ strictly exceeds $c$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For $B$, $2a+2b+3c>c$ is trivial. For $A$, $2a-2b+3c>c$ reduces to $a+c>b$, true since $b<c$. For $C$, $-2a+2b+3c>c$ reduces to $b+c>a$, true since $a<c$.
\end{proof}

\section{The uniform parent map and the descent theorem}

\begin{definition}\label{def:parent}
For a triple $(a,b,c)$ set
\[ u=a+2b-2c,\qquad v=2a+b-2c,\qquad h=3c-2a-2b, \]
and define the \emph{parent map} $P(a,b,c)=(|u|,|v|,h)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Reconstruction]\label{lem:recon}
Identically in $(a,b,c)$: $a=u+2v+2h$, $b=2u+v+2h$, $c=2u+2v+3h$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Direct substitution and expansion.
\end{proof}

Thus $(u,v,h)\mapsto(a,b,c)$ is exactly the move $B$: the parent map is $B^{-1}$ followed by absolute values, and the absolute values encode the branch.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:parpyth}
If $\Pyth(a,b,c)$ then $\Pyth(u,v,h)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$u^2+v^2-h^2=a^2+b^2-c^2$ as a polynomial identity.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Positivity and strict decrease]\label{lem:hbounds}
If $\PPT(a,b,c)$ then $0<h<c$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For $h>0$: since $a^2+b^2=c^2$ we get $(a+b)^2=c^2+2ab\le 2c^2$, so $2(a+b)\le 2\sqrt2\,c<3c$. For $h<c$: $3c-2a-2b<c$ is $c<a+b$, which holds because $c^2=a^2+b^2<(a+b)^2$ for positive $a,b$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Sign behaviour]\label{lem:signs}
Let $\PPT(a,b,c)$. Then
\begin{enumerate}
\item $u$ is odd, hence $u\neq 0$;
\item if $c>5$ then $v\neq 0$;
\item $u>0$ or $v>0$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
(1) $u=a+2b-2c\equiv a\equiv 1 \pmod 2$.

(2) If $v=0$ then $2a+b=2c$; substituting into $a^2+b^2=c^2$ yields $3b=4a$, so $3\mid a$, say $a=3t$, $b=4t$; coprimality forces $t=1$, i.e.\ $(a,b,c)=(3,4,5)$, contradicting $c>5$.

(3) Suppose $u\le 0$ and $v\le 0$, i.e.\ $a+2b\le 2c$ and $2a+b\le 2c$, all quantities positive. Squaring and using $c^2=a^2+b^2$,
\[ a^2+4ab+4b^2\le 4a^2+4b^2 \Rightarrow 4ab\le 3a^2, \qquad 4a^2+4ab+b^2\le 4a^2+4b^2 \Rightarrow 4ab\le 3b^2 . \]
Multiplying gives $16a^2b^2\le 9a^2b^2$, impossible since $ab>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The parent map is an inverse Berggren move]\label{thm:parenteqinv}
Let $\PPT(a,b,c)$ with $c>5$. Then
\[ P(a,b,c)=\begin{cases} A^{-1}(a,b,c) & \text{if } u>0>v,\\ B^{-1}(a,b,c) & \text{if } u>0 \text{ and } v>0,\\ C^{-1}(a,b,c) & \text{if } u<0<v,\end{cases} \]
and by Lemma~\ref{lem:signs} these cases are exhaustive and mutually exclusive.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Unfolding, $A^{-1}=(u,-v,h)$, $B^{-1}=(u,v,h)$, $C^{-1}=(-u,v,h)$. Now $(|u|,|v|,h)$ equals $(u,-v,h)$ when $u>0>v$, equals $(u,v,h)$ when both are positive, and equals $(-u,v,h)$ when $u<0<v$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Descent preserves the class]\label{thm:parentppt}
If $\PPT(a,b,c)$ and $c>5$ then $\PPT(P(a,b,c))$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Positivity of the first two coordinates is $u\neq0$, $v\neq0$ (Lemma~\ref{lem:signs}) plus absolute values; positivity of the third is Lemma~\ref{lem:hbounds}. The Pythagorean equation is Lemma~\ref{lem:parpyth} together with $|x|^2=x^2$. Coprimality: apply Lemma~\ref{lem:coptransfer} to $(u,v,h)$ using the reconstruction of Lemma~\ref{lem:recon}, and note $\gcd(|u|,|v|)=\gcd(u,v)$. Oddness: $|u|\equiv u\equiv a \pmod 2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Well-founded descent]\label{thm:wf}
If $\PPT(a,b,c)$ then the third coordinate of $P(a,b,c)$ is $h$ with $0<h<c$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Lemma~\ref{lem:hbounds}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Child recovery]\label{thm:childrec}
If $\PPT(a,b,c)$ and $c>5$ then $(a,b,c)$ equals $A(P(a,b,c))$, $B(P(a,b,c))$ or $C(P(a,b,c))$, according to the sign trichotomy of Theorem~\ref{thm:parenteqinv}.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Each case is the identity $X(X^{-1}(a,b,c))=(a,b,c)$ for $X\in\{A,B,C\}$, i.e.\ matrix inversion.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Descent theorem]\label{thm:descent}
Every normalised primitive triple lies in $\Reach$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Strong induction on $c$. If $c\le5$ the triple is $(3,4,5)$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:root}. Otherwise $P(a,b,c)$ is normalised primitive (Theorem~\ref{thm:parentppt}) with strictly smaller positive hypotenuse (Theorem~\ref{thm:wf}), hence in $\Reach$ by induction, and $(a,b,c)$ is one of its Berggren children (Theorem~\ref{thm:childrec}).
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Classification of the Berggren tree]\label{thm:classification}
$(a,b,c)\in\Reach \iff \PPT(a,b,c)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\Rightarrow$) Induction on the generation of $\Reach$, using $\PPT(3,4,5)$ and Proposition~\ref{prop:forward}. ($\Leftarrow$) Theorem~\ref{thm:descent}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Decidability of membership]\label{cor:decid}
Membership in the Berggren tree is decidable by the elementary test ``$a,b,c>0$ and $a^2+b^2=c^2$ and $\gcd(a,b)=1$ and $a$ odd''. No search over the tree is required.
\end{corollary}

\section{The address map and freeness of the tree}

\begin{definition}
Let $\Sigma=\{A,B,C\}$ and $\Sigma^*$ the free monoid of finite words. Define
\[ \addr:\Sigma^*\to\Z^3,\qquad \addr(\varepsilon)=(3,4,5),\qquad \addr(s\,w)=s\bigl(\addr(w)\bigr). \]
The word is read right-to-left from the root; the \emph{first} letter is the last move applied. The \emph{depth} of a triple $t$ is $d(t)=|w|$ where $\addr(w)=t$ (well defined by Theorem~\ref{thm:free}).
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[$u,v$ read off the last move]\label{lem:readoff}
For all $a,b,c$,
\[
\begin{array}{lll}
u(A(a,b,c))=a, & v(A(a,b,c))=-b, & h(A(a,b,c))=c,\\
u(B(a,b,c))=a, & v(B(a,b,c))=b, & h(B(a,b,c))=c,\\
u(C(a,b,c))=-a, & v(C(a,b,c))=b, & h(C(a,b,c))=c.
\end{array}
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Nine polynomial identities, each a one-line expansion.
\end{proof}

Lemma~\ref{lem:readoff} makes the descent transparent: applying $u,v,h$ to a child returns the parent's coordinates \emph{up to sign}, and the sign pattern $(+,-)$, $(+,+)$, $(-,+)$ is precisely the label $A$, $B$, $C$ of the move used.

\begin{corollary}[The parent map deletes the head letter]\label{cor:delete}
For $a,b>0$ and $X\in\{A,B,C\}$ we have $P(X(a,b,c))=(a,b,c)$; hence $P(\addr(s\,w))=\addr(w)$ for all $s\in\Sigma$, $w\in\Sigma^*$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{lemma}[Children are distinct]\label{lem:childdistinct}
If $\PPT(a,b,c)$ then $A(a,b,c)$, $B(a,b,c)$, $C(a,b,c)$ are pairwise distinct.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Comparing first coordinates: $A$ and $B$ agree iff $b=0$; $B$ and $C$ agree iff $a=0$; $A$ and $C$ agree iff $a=2b$, impossible since $a$ is odd.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Freeness of the Berggren tree]\label{thm:free}
The map $\addr$ is injective with image exactly $\{t:\PPT(t)\}$. Hence every normalised primitive triple has a unique address and
\[ \addr:\Sigma^*\ \xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }\ \{\text{normalised primitive Pythagorean triples}\} \]
is a bijection, computable in both directions.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
\emph{Image.} $\addr(w)$ is normalised primitive by induction (Proposition~\ref{prop:forward}); conversely every such triple is reached (Theorem~\ref{thm:descent}), and an induction over the generation of $\Reach$ produces a word.

\emph{Injectivity.} Since the moves strictly increase the hypotenuse and every triple has $c\ge5$ (Lemmas~\ref{lem:root},~\ref{lem:hypup}), $\addr(sw)$ has hypotenuse $>5$ and cannot equal $\addr(\varepsilon)=(3,4,5)$. For $\addr(sw)=\addr(tw')$, apply $P$ and use Corollary~\ref{cor:delete} to get $\addr(w)=\addr(w')$, hence $w=w'$ by induction; then $s=t$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:childdistinct}.

\emph{Computable inverse.} Iterate $P$, recording at each step the sign pattern of $(u,v)$; the process halts at $(3,4,5)$ after $d(t)$ steps by Theorem~\ref{thm:wf}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Answer to (Q1)]\label{cor:noQ1}
The relation $\{(w,t):\addr(w)=t\}$ is decidable and $\Sigma^*$ acts freely: no nontrivial relations hold among the generators. There is therefore no Diophantine encoding to be extracted from the tree's address function.
\end{corollary}

\begin{example}
$\addr(\varepsilon)=(3,4,5)$, $\addr(B)=(21,20,29)$, $\addr(BB)=(119,120,169)$, $\addr(A)=(5,12,13)$, $\addr(C)=(15,8,17)$. Conversely from $(7,24,25)$: $u=5>0$, $v=-12<0$, so the last move was $A$ and the parent is $(5,12,13)$; from $(5,12,13)$: $u=3>0$, $v=-4<0$, again $A$, parent $(3,4,5)$. Address $AA$, depth $2$.
\end{example}

\section{The Pythagorean Hydra}

\begin{definition}[Berggren ancestry]
For triples $s,t$ write $s\prec t$ if $\PPT(t)$, the hypotenuse of $t$ exceeds $5$, and $s=P(t)$. Let $\prec^{+}$ be the transitive closure: $s$ is a \emph{Berggren ancestor} of $t$.
\end{definition}

By Theorem~\ref{thm:wf} and Corollary~\ref{cor:delete}, $s\prec t$ implies $d(s)+1=d(t)$ and $c(s)<c(t)$; hence $s\prec^{+}t$ implies $d(s)<d(t)$.

\begin{definition}[The game]
A \emph{hydra} is a finite multiset $H$ of normalised primitive triples. A \emph{chop with branching bound $k$} replaces $H=t\uplus H_0$ by $R\uplus H_0$, where $R$ is a multiset of at most $k$ triples, each a Berggren ancestor of $t$. A \emph{battle of length $N$} is a chain of $N$ chops. Hercules \emph{wins} if every battle is finite. The \emph{unbounded} game drops the condition $|R|\le k$.
\end{definition}

The root $(3,4,5)$ is inert: it has no Berggren ancestor, since any ancestor would be a normalised primitive triple of hypotenuse $<5$, contradicting Lemma~\ref{lem:root}.

\begin{theorem}[Hercules always wins]\label{thm:terminates}
There is no infinite battle, even in the unbounded game.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Map each head to its depth. A Pythagorean chop becomes an abstract move replacing a natural number by finitely many strictly smaller ones, i.e.\ a strict decrease in the Dershowitz--Manna multiset order on finite multisets of naturals, which is well founded because $(\N,<)$ is.
\end{proof}

The multiset order on finite multisets of naturals has order type $\omega^\omega$; the next section shows this is exact, and that the bounded game is elementary.

\section{Exact length function and calibration}

\begin{definition}[Potential]
For $k,n\in\N$ put $\varphi_k(n)=\sum_{i=0}^{n}k^i=1+k+\cdots+k^n$, and for a hydra $H$,
\[ \Phi_k(H)=\sum_{t\in H}\varphi_k\bigl(d(t)\bigr). \]
\end{definition}

Basic facts: $\varphi_k(0)=1$; $\varphi_k(n+1)=1+k\varphi_k(n)$; $\varphi_k$ is nondecreasing; $\varphi_k(n)\le (k+1)^{n+1}$.

\begin{lemma}[Each move costs at least one unit]\label{lem:drop}
If $H$ chops to $H'$ with branching bound $k$, then $\Phi_k(H')+1\le\Phi_k(H)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If the chopped head has depth $m$ and $R$ is the regrown multiset ($|R|\le k$, all depths $\le m-1$), then
\[ \Phi_k(H)-\Phi_k(H')=\varphi_k(m)-\sum_{s\in R}\varphi_k(d(s))\ \ge\ \varphi_k(m)-k\varphi_k(m-1)=1 . \]
If $m=0$ then $R$ is empty and the drop is $\varphi_k(0)=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The length function of the Pythagorean Hydra]\label{thm:length}
Fix $k$ and a hydra $H$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item Every battle from $H$ with branching bound $k$ has at most $\Phi_k(H)$ moves.
\item There is a battle from $H$ of length exactly $\Phi_k(H)$ ending with the empty hydra.
\end{enumerate}
Hence the longest battle from $H$ has exactly $\Phi_k(H)$ moves.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
(1) By Lemma~\ref{lem:drop} and induction, after $N$ moves $N+\Phi_k(H_N)\le\Phi_k(H)$, and $\Phi_k\ge0$.

(2) It suffices that every non-empty hydra admits a move dropping $\Phi_k$ by exactly one; then induction on $\Phi_k(H)$ gives a battle of that length ending empty (note $\Phi_k(H)=0$ iff $H=\emptyset$, as $\varphi_k\ge1$). Pick a head $t$. If $d(t)=0$, i.e.\ $t=(3,4,5)$, chop and regrow nothing: the drop is $1$. Otherwise regrow $k$ copies of the parent $P(t)$, of depth $d(t)-1$: the drop is $\varphi_k(d(t))-k\varphi_k(d(t)-1)=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Single head]
A battle against the single head $\addr(w)$, of depth $d=|w|$, with branching bound $k$, lasts at most $\varphi_k(d)=1+k+\cdots+k^d\le (k+1)^{d+1}$ moves, and this is attained.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Root]\label{cor:root}
A battle against the single head $(3,4,5)$ lasts at most one move, for every $k$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Hypotenuse form]
Measuring levels by the hypotenuse rather than the depth still yields a valid, weaker bound: if every head has hypotenuse at most $L$ then a battle with branching bound $k$ lasts at most $|H|\,(k+1)^{L+1}$ moves. For the single head $(3,4,5)$ with $k=3$ this reads $N\le\varphi_3(5)=364$, against the sharp bound $1$ of Corollary~\ref{cor:root}: the gap measures how lossy the hypotenuse is as a proxy for depth.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Unbounded regrowth]\label{thm:unbounded}
In the unbounded game every battle is finite, but for every $N$ there is a battle of length $N+1$ from a single head of level $1$ (chop it, regrow $N$ heads of level $0$, then chop those). Hence the length is not a function of the initial hydra: the game's ordinal is strictly greater than $\omega$, and by Theorem~\ref{thm:terminates} it is exactly $\omega^\omega$.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Calibration: why there is no independence phenomenon}

\begin{theorem}[Calibration; answer to (Q2)]\label{thm:calibration}
The termination of the Pythagorean Hydra with branching bound $k$ is witnessed by the explicit elementary function $\Phi_k$, which strictly decreases at every move. Consequently: the statement ``every battle terminates'' is provable by induction on a single natural-number parameter, well inside Peano Arithmetic; the length function $H\mapsto\Phi_k(H)$ is elementary, bounded by $|H|(k+1)^{L+1}$, and therefore does not majorise the provably total functions of $\mathrm{PA}$; and the ordinal of the game is $\omega^\omega$, as against the $\varepsilon_0$ of the Kirby--Paris hydra. The Pythagorean Hydra is not a natural independence phenomenon.
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}
The structural reason is worth stating precisely. In the Kirby--Paris game each head is a \emph{tree}, and the ordinal of a hydra is built by nesting: a node with children of ordinals $\alpha_1,\dots,\alpha_r$ receives $\omega^{\alpha_1}+\cdots+\omega^{\alpha_r}$, and regrowth copies whole subtrees, so the notation must accommodate towers $\omega^{\omega^{\cdots}}$, i.e.\ all of $\varepsilon_0$. In the Berggren tree each head is a \emph{word}, and the descent invariant is a single natural number, its depth; a hydra is thus a finite multiset of naturals, of order type $\omega^\omega$. The Berggren tree is \emph{flat} as an ordinal notation. Independence among Pythagorean triples, if it exists, must come from a rule that builds \emph{height}, not merely branching.
\end{remark}

\section{Sharpness: descent is exactly what makes Hercules win}

\begin{theorem}[Non-strict descent fails]\label{thm:relaxed}
In the relaxed game where a regrown head may have level $\le$ that of the chopped head, there is an infinite play: the hydra $\{1\}$ chops its head and regrows one head at level $1$, forever.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Reversed regrowth fails]\label{thm:children}
In the variant where chopping $t$ permits regrowth of Berggren \emph{children} of $t$, there is an explicit infinite battle along the $B$-spine:
\[ \{(3,4,5)\}\to\{(21,20,29)\}\to\{(119,120,169)\}\to\{(697,696,985)\}\to\cdots, \]
the hydra at stage $i$ being $\{\addr(B^i)\}$.
\end{theorem}

Together, Theorems~\ref{thm:relaxed} and~\ref{thm:children} show that the Pythagorean Hydra sits exactly on the boundary of termination: strict Berggren descent is both sufficient (Theorem~\ref{thm:terminates}) and necessary. It is the \emph{direction} of the regrowth --- towards $(3,4,5)$ --- and nothing else, that makes the game finite.

\section{Algorithms}

\textbf{Descent / address extraction.} Given $(a,b,c)$, verify $\PPT$ by Corollary~\ref{cor:decid}; then while $c>5$ compute $u,v,h$, append $A$, $B$ or $C$ according to the sign pattern $(+,-)$, $(+,+)$, $(-,+)$, and replace $(a,b,c)$ by $(|u|,|v|,h)$. Halt at $(3,4,5)$. Termination is Theorem~\ref{thm:wf}; correctness is Theorem~\ref{thm:parenteqinv} with Lemma~\ref{lem:readoff}. The loop runs exactly $d(a,b,c)$ times, which is $O(\sqrt{c})$: along the $A$-spine $(3,4,5)\to(5,12,13)\to(7,24,25)\to\cdots$ the node at depth $d$ is $(2d+3,\,2d^2+6d+4,\,2d^2+6d+5)$, so depth grows like $\sqrt{c/2}$, the extreme case; along the $B$-spine the hypotenuse is multiplied by roughly $3+2\sqrt2\approx5.83$ per step, giving depth $O(\log c)$.

\textbf{Maximal battle.} Repeat: choose any head $t$; if $t=(3,4,5)$ chop and regrow nothing, else chop and regrow $k$ copies of $P(t)$. By the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:length} the potential drops by exactly one per move, so the battle has $\Phi_k(H)$ moves and ends empty.

\textbf{Potential evaluation.} $\Phi_k(H)=\sum_{t\in H}\varphi_k(d(t))$ with $\varphi_k(d)=(k^{d+1}-1)/(k-1)$ for $k\ne1$ and $d+1$ for $k=1$: the exact game length without simulation.

\section{Applications and discussion}

\textbf{Enumeration and coding.} Freeness gives a canonical bijective coding of primitive triples by ternary strings. Enumerating all primitive triples with hypotenuse below a bound becomes a breadth-first search needing no duplicate check, because the tree is free and the hypotenuse strictly increases along every edge (Lemma~\ref{lem:hypup}).

\textbf{A normal form for descent.} The classical three-case argument is replaced by one formula with absolute values: the branch is a byproduct, not a precondition.

\textbf{Calibration as a design tool.} Theorem~\ref{thm:calibration} tells us what a candidate independence phenomenon on arithmetic structures must look like. Any structure whose descent yields a single natural-number rank per object supports at most $\omega^\omega$. The hierarchy --- $\omega^\omega$ for multisets of naturals, $\varepsilon_0$ for hereditarily finite trees --- is a statement about how much \emph{nesting} the objects carry, not about arithmetic depth. Pythagorean triples are arithmetically rich but ordinally shallow.

Both moonshot conjectures were refuted, and the refutations are theorems rather than obstructions. For undecidability the obstruction is \emph{freeness}: equality of two addresses is equality of words. For independence the obstruction is \emph{flatness}. It is instructive how close the analogy comes before it breaks: the tree is infinite and ternary, regrowth by ancestors mimics Kirby--Paris regrowth, termination genuinely needs a well-foundedness argument, and under unbounded branching it genuinely needs the multiset order (Theorem~\ref{thm:unbounded}). All the ingredients are present \emph{except} the nesting that lifts $\omega^\omega$ to $\varepsilon_0$.

There is a positive reading too. The closed form $\Phi_k(H)=\sum_t(1+k+\cdots+k^{d(t)})$ is rare: a hydra game whose longest battle is known exactly, attained, and computable without simulation --- all the phenomenology of hydra games with none of the transfinite machinery.

\section{Future directions}

\textbf{Iterated-tree hydras.} A Kirby--Paris hydra needs regrowth copying subtrees of unbounded height, and the Berggren tree supplies such subtrees canonically: the address of a node is a word, and words can be re-read as addresses, giving ``triples of triples''. A hydra whose heads are Berggren words of Berggren words has ordinal $\omega^{\omega^\omega}$; iterating approaches $\varepsilon_0$. Finding an \emph{arithmetically natural} regrowth rule at each level is the obvious next step.

\textbf{Bounded-depth variants.} Parametrising the game by a function controlling regrowth at stage $n$ (as in the Kirby--Paris rule) changes the length function but not the ordinal for a flat game; determining the exact growth rates would sharpen Theorem~\ref{thm:calibration} into a hierarchy theorem.

\textbf{Other descent structures.} Markov triples, the Stern--Brocot tree, and Vieta-jumping trees of quadratic Diophantine equations all admit uniform parent maps analogous to $P$; the calibration applies verbatim, suggesting a general theorem that every Vieta-jumping descent structure yields a flat hydra.

\textbf{Metric questions.} The depth of a triple of hypotenuse $c$ ranges from $\Theta(\log c)$ (the $B$-spine) to $\Theta(\sqrt c)$ (the $A$-spine). What is the \emph{average} depth of a primitive triple with hypotenuse at most $X$, and how are the letters distributed along a random address? Numerically, among the $15919$ nodes of hypotenuse at most $10^5$ the depth distribution peaks at depth $8$ and decays slowly, with a long tail reaching depth $222$ --- the node $(447,99904,99905)$ on the $A$-spine.

\section{Conclusion}

We have given a complete descent theory for the Berggren tree via a single uniform parent map whose absolute values encode the branch, deduced the classification and decidability of the tree, proved the address map to be a computable bijection from the free monoid on three letters, and defined and completely analysed the Pythagorean Hydra: it terminates, its longest battle has exactly $\sum_{t\in H}(1+k+\cdots+k^{d(t)})$ moves, and both relaxing and reversing the descent rule destroy termination outright. Neither the hoped-for undecidability nor the hoped-for independence phenomenon exists; what exists instead is an exact calibration, locating the arithmetic of Pythagorean triples firmly inside the reach of Peano Arithmetic and pointing to iterated addressing as the only plausible route beyond it.

\end{document}

